+# Weighted SwiGLU in BI-V150 MoE training
+
+4L H512 FFN2048 S128 V1024 batch2 experts4 topk2 SequentialMLP; 55,652,864 parameters
+
+Native is pinned local Torch Megatron with default compiled weighted SwiGLU, native RMSNorm and fused causal softmax=True. Control adds only CE0008 and gradnorm001; optimized adds weighted SwiGLU004. Dense SwiGLU004, Attention003, GroupedGEMM and candidate router/GEMM kernels are absent. The explicit common bool-to-exact0/1-uint8 stable-sort compatibility was admitted by 32 CPU-reference index probes in stage33. Original GPU bool stable sort is unavailable; all three arms use the same compatibility.
+
+BF16 model/intermediates, FP32 main_grad and SGD master, mock data, dropout0, no autocast, TP/PP/CP/EP1. Actual weighted input is BF16[N,4096], probabilities BF16[N,1], bias=None, fp8_store=False; expert token counts 38–256 in the three-update native capture. Auxiliary router-loss coefficient 0.01 is retained.
+
+## Full three-arm timing
+
+| Seed | Native ms/step | CE+gradnorm ms/step | +weighted SwiGLU004 ms/step |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+|34102|72.32|69.64|67.35|
+|34103|74.15|70.00|66.96|
+|34104|72.89|72.76|66.47|
+
+Decision after both independent rounds: `promoted_tested_model_only`.
+
+- Weighted activation increment over same-round CE+gradnorm control: +5.7686%; all seeds faster: True.
+- Combined CE+gradnorm+weighted activation over same-round native: +9.2495%; all seeds faster: True.
+
+The first complete round measured +17.0277% versus control and +26.1933% versus native. Some native/control arms had larger variance (native73.77/86.03/100.71ms; control93.94/75.45/71.92ms), so an independent complete nine-process repeat used IDENTICAL controller/candidate bytes and the same 3 seeds/orders/120-step timer. The table above is that repeat; both original rounds remain archived. Promotion requires both rounds to pass the original >1%/all3-faster rule.
+
+Separate 20-step profiles (not formal timing) record 220 Triton compile calls for 003 and 11 for 004. Original cProfile data is retained locally and hash-bound; the public bundle includes original parsed counters and receipts.
+
+## Numerical boundaries and failed iterations
+
+The fixed independent probe has 44 cases (empty, unit, probability/signed weights, widths257/769/2048, actual expert replay, fresh inputs and nondefault streams) plus 2 CPU/FP32 guards. The main probe clones its inputs; its offset-labelled rows do not establish actual pointer-offset coverage. A separate preserved-view probe adds 2 genuine contiguous offset1/mod16=2 cases, without altering the original probe or tolerance. All 48 original expert calls are replayed against their captured upstream gradients; native replay must be bitwise before candidate comparison.
+
+Full three-update native/control/optimized comparisons each contain 2627 checks and 1289 tensors, including all 56 FP32 gradient/master sets, BF16 parameters, original batches/losses,12 router maps/probabilities and 12 probability gradients. Tolerances stay CPU-float64 atol3e-4/rtol1e-3. Candidate004 passes the complete scope; bitwise status is recorded in the CPU proof. Three-update equality does not prove 120-step tensor equivalence or convergence.
+
+Candidate001 passed the small probe but failed 2 step 3 embedding-gradient tensors (8 elements outside tolerance). All 48 native replays isolate one weights_grad BF16 rounding boundary at call 47 N97 (abs9.5367e-7); output and dx are bitwise on all 48. Candidate002 replaced this reduction with a standalone torch.compile function and passed training bitwise, but full nine-process timing regressed: control71–73ms versus candidate157–784ms/step. It is not promoted; original records are retained. Candidate003 mirrors the retained original Inductor two-dimensional accumulator loop (XBLOCK1/RBLOCK2048/8warps/1stage forH2048) and restores immutable compiled-launch closure reuse with current runtime arguments and streams, but its exact N/grid keys still trigger vendor shape-specific compilation. Its complete three-seed training also regresses (optimized162–783ms/step versus control70–73ms/step), and it is not promoted. An independent 20-step host profile observes 220 Triton compile calls in 268 launcher calls. The installed vendor JIT includes tensor shapes in its cache key. Candidate004 instead caches immutable CompiledKernel objects across dynamic N and grids, keeps runtime sizes unspecialized, and launches each binary with the current grid/tensors/stream. Only compiled binaries are cached, never input/output tensors; unsupported inputs preserve the native callback. The captured compiler-cache catalog contains both original native and candidate002-generated entries; the selected original weighted-backward wrapper and reduction are separately hash-bound, not part of the 600 original source manifest.
+
+Primary timing remains the unchanged native GPU-synchronized time.time loop: two complete rounds, each 3 seeds/3 rotated arms/120 steps per process, first 20 excluded, ten retained interval-10 means. No profiling, tensor audit, checkpoint, evaluation or large backups during timing. Promotion requires >1% geometric throughput versus same-round control and every seed faster. CUDA Event operator numbers include host/autograd submission gaps and are kept separately. CPU/CI verifies raw receipt coverage/hashes/statistics, never reruns GPU arithmetic; unavailable large raw bytes remain explicit in CI. Complete local raw inventory also preserves failed-candidate and diagnostic snapshots.
+
+This does not validate production training, AdamW, GroupedGEMM, multiGPU, RL/SFT, convergence, or NVIDIA TMEM/TMA/tcgen05/WGMMA/NVFP4 instruction equivalents. Do not transfer NVIDIA performance claims to BI-V150.
+
+## Pinned native oracle snippet
+
+```python
+import torch
+from megatron.core.fusions.fused_bias_swiglu import weighted_bias_swiglu_impl
+x = torch.randn(97, 4096, device="cuda", dtype=torch.bfloat16, requires_grad=True)
+w = torch.rand(97, 1, device="cuda", dtype=torch.bfloat16, requires_grad=True)
+y = weighted_bias_swiglu_impl(x, None, w, False)
+dx, dw = torch.autograd.grad(y, (x, w), torch.randn_like(y))
+assert dx.dtype == dw.dtype == torch.bfloat16
+```
